  {
    "index": 3,
    "hash": "3fVRl3sn3NRPU2zMvkj6GAyN7Js=",
    "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-19T11:29:05.839Z",
    "action": {
      "type": "SET_CONTENT",
      "input": {
        "content": "inovex is around 500 people, runs roughly 90% of its projects on open source, and works in Kubernetes and edge/automotive. The open-source share matters as a screening signal: adopting an openly licensed component is normal there rather than exceptional, which removes a class of objection before it appears.\n\nSize changes what fit means. At this scale a single client integration is not the interesting unit \u2014 it is one project among many and vanishes into the portfolio. The research states the alternative plainly: the fit is a reusable delivery asset that many engagements draw on, which is how a large consultancy turns a technology into revenue.\n\nThat framing has consequences for how the lead is worked. A reusable asset needs an internal owner and a standardisation decision, not just an interested engineer, so progress is measured in adoption inside the firm rather than in a first deployment. It is slower than the small-partner pattern elsewhere in the corpus, and more durable if it lands.\n\n---\n\n## Leads sharing this finding (1)\n\n**2 \u00b7 next**\n- inovex GmbH (DE Karlsruhe +6 + CH Bern) \u2014 Stephan M\u00fcller + Tobias Joch, Gesch\u00e4ftsf\u00fchrer \u00b7 info@inovex.de \u00b7 https://inovex.de\n\nVerbatim research text this claim was extracted from:\n\n> ~500 staff, ~90% of projects on open source, Kubernetes and edge/automotive. At this size the fit is a reusable delivery asset across many client engagements, not a single integration.",
        "updatedAt": "2026-08-19T11:29:05.839364Z"
      },
      "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-19T11:29:05.839368Z"
    }
  },
